#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357620 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2d7d4db-e9e1-54a0-aadb-42447771855d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain Where All Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba0dd6c4-2ec2-57c6-8815-05889f234bfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0,\\,y>0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357622} {:answer/id #uuid "5c500de6-2fa9-5d6b-841b-8c95e9a1a955" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ge 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357623} {:answer/id #uuid "7c88e736-7fe7-5d68-a0f8-2e634096df87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y\\ge 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357624} {:answer/id #uuid "4c223153-6e30-52d6-89a4-a6c5de13c698" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357625} {:answer/id #uuid "842dfa9d-2cc2-52a2-a6b8-25831f13af5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "842dfa9d-2cc2-52a2-a6b8-25831f13af5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357626} :answer-field/id #uuid "563f6f99-2f00-53cb-a98f-49b5a20f7fe3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b3b5f309-b99a-58d2-a8fe-7b4b39ba90d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-132649" :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)=\\sqrt{y}\\,{e}^{x/y}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{{e}^{x/y}}{\\sqrt{y}},\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{(y-2x)\\,{e}^{x/y}}{2\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ where all partial derivatives of $g$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $g(x,y)=\\sqrt{y}\\,{e}^{x/y}$ is defined only when $y>0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}={(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{x/y}}{\\sqrt{y}}$ is defined only when $y>0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domain of ${g}_{y}(x,y)$ is the same: \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ge 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357627 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33a8a9a0-faf8-5a03-8622-2faded5390dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357629} {:answer/id #uuid "345f5957-0bcd-51c3-badb-af36ed7f1015" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357630} {:answer/id #uuid "ad12eb88-9f8c-5d7c-ad2f-08dc9e8ca1d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357631} {:answer/id #uuid "33dca3b4-aee9-53ff-b664-877da90970c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357632} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e54042-06cd-564f-abd3-b3074eaa9049" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "345f5957-0bcd-51c3-badb-af36ed7f1015" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357630} :answer-field/id #uuid "de7880cd-7fed-5374-8a13-fdda113d2760" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c080c662-33ec-504b-a707-6014b82b52d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-132680" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}?$\n\n- Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(4,2)$\n- ${f}_{x}(0,2)$ is defined\n- ${f}_{y}(0,2)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(4,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(4,2)=\\frac{\\sqrt{4}}{{2}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(4,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(4,2) & =\\frac{1}{2({2}^{2})\\sqrt{4}}=\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ {f}_{y}(4,2) & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{4}}{{2}^{3}}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(0,2)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,2)=\\frac{1}{2({2}^{2})\\sqrt{0}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $f$ itself is defined at $(0,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,2)=\\frac{\\sqrt{0}}{{2}^{2}}=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(0,2)=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{0}}{{2}^{3}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125109 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2d7d4db-e9e1-54a0-aadb-42447771855d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain Where All Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7dc10c7-157a-5f30-8a86-824714cacf1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y): xy < 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123677} {:answer/id #uuid "b8381050-42b8-52bb-8246-566f0dbacb26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y): xy \\ge 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123678} {:answer/id #uuid "e1887954-c0f1-5774-91e7-bc9210947fc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y): xy > 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123679} {:answer/id #uuid "2fb8c04c-acb7-5d38-a504-5c395fd810c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y): y > 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123680} {:answer/id #uuid "6322a37f-4280-52cb-809f-3d14b088f1ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y): y \\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1887954-c0f1-5774-91e7-bc9210947fc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y): xy > 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123679} :answer-field/id #uuid "71715649-d424-5f87-9bdf-f1bcf7cce73b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0790aa49-4020-5e37-b9fd-2a18a4aa638d" :question/math-academy-id "q-65252" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ where all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ is defined only when $xy>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}(x,y)=1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ is defined only when $xy>0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357634 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "744b6a87-6f2e-588e-b10c-e55c12780637" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357636} {:answer/id #uuid "1d181cc5-f783-5479-acd7-2e5579af25be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357637} {:answer/id #uuid "1a048517-a609-58c6-ad38-a87de9c09e42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357638} {:answer/id #uuid "7469fe96-f4f4-54bb-8f90-edf74b686f53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357639} {:answer/id #uuid "6a91ebb6-4ec8-50ba-b8c0-ff933b26e90e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d181cc5-f783-5479-acd7-2e5579af25be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357637} :answer-field/id #uuid "15eef8f5-247a-578a-a9bf-38f18c5e82fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357635}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24bd2a92-ff74-539f-a205-0ce34162c268" :question/math-academy-id "q-65255" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=xy{e}^{x/y}?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(0,1)$is defined\n- ${f}_{y}(1,0)$ is defined\n- Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,1)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy{e}^{x/y}) \\\\  & =y{e}^{x/y}+xy{e}^{x/y}(\\frac{1}{y}) \\\\  & =y{e}^{x/y}+x{e}^{x/y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy{e}^{x/y}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{x/y}+xy{e}^{x/y}(-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{x/y}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}{e}^{x/y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ itself is defined at $(0,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,1)=0\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{0/1}=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1)=1\\cdot {e}^{0/1}+0\\cdot {e}^{0/1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0)$ into ${f}_{y}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{y}(1,0)=1\\cdot {e}^{1/0}-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{0}\\cdot {e}^{1/0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,1)=1\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}=e,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}+1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}=2e \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,1) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{1}\\cdot {e}^{1/1}=e-e=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357641 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2d7d4db-e9e1-54a0-aadb-42447771855d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain Where All Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "deec98cc-0ef7-53dc-b7ea-90eaf0c02432" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357643} {:answer/id #uuid "9e076db3-2140-52ed-8dbd-14cfe1650587" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\ne y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357644} {:answer/id #uuid "dc2b169c-2198-51aa-8898-32b25f075fc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\le y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357645} {:answer/id #uuid "58a11d90-6465-5804-8fd7-983351883327" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}>y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357646} {:answer/id #uuid "d0cf2cb2-0c4b-50f3-9aaf-90fba144644b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\ge y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "deec98cc-0ef7-53dc-b7ea-90eaf0c02432" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357643} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f4b9151-cd88-54db-8de1-75d6eb4473bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78f42681-eb03-567c-9137-705306bec321" :question/math-academy-id "q-72027" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{1+y-{x}^{2}}}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{xz}{\\sqrt{(1+y-{x}^{2})^{3}}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{z}{2\\sqrt{(1+y-{x}^{2})^{3}}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+y-{x}^{2}}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{1+y-{x}^{2}}}$ is defined only when $1+y-{x}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}=\\frac{xz}{\\sqrt{(1+y-{x}^{2})^{3}}}$ is defined only when $1+y-{x}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ and ${f}_{z}$ are the same\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={D}_{{f}_{z}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
